1 CONC United States v. CERTAIN PARCELS OF LAND, ETC., 149 F. Supp. 696 (N.D
2 CONC . Ohio 1957)
2 CONT
2 CONT U.S. District Court for the Northern District of Ohio - 149 F. Supp. 6
2 CONC 96 (N.D. Ohio 1957)
2 CONT March 22, 1957
2 CONT 149 F. Supp. 696 (1957)
2 CONT UNITED STATES of America, Petitioner,
2 CONT v.
2 CONT CERTAIN PARCELS OF LAND IN the CITY OF CLEVELAND, COUNTY OF CUYAHOGA
2 CONC , STATE OF OHIO; Grabler Manufacturing Co., et al., Defendants.
2 CONT No. 22015.
2 CONT
2 CONT United States District Court N. D. Ohio, E. D.
2 CONT March 22, 1957.
2 CONT
2 CONT *697 *698 Sumner Canary, U. S. Dist. Atty., Russell E. Ake, Asst. U. S
2 CONC . Dist. Atty., Cleveland, Ohio, for petitioner.
2 CONT
2 CONT Morris Berick, Cleveland, Ohio, for movant, Jacob Hecht.
2 CONT
2 CONT WEICK, District Judge.
2 CONT
2 CONT The original petition in condemnation was filed on October 1, 1943 a
2 CONC t the request of the Commissioner of Public Housing, to obtain propert
2 CONC y to provide housing for persons engaged in national defense activitie
2 CONC s and their families in the Cleveland area. An order for the immediat
2 CONC e possession of the property was entered by the Court on the same day.
2 CONT
2 CONT The estate sought to be appropriated was the exclusive use of vacant l
2 CONC and for a period of one year commencing October *699 1, 1943 with th
2 CONC e right of the Government to renew from year to year for the duratio
2 CONC n of the war emergency and for three years thereafter and to remove al
2 CONC l improvements placed thereon by it at the termination of such use.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Declaration of Taking was filed on November 29, 1943 and on the sa
2 CONC me day a decree was entered thereon by the Court.
2 CONT
2 CONT Parcel No. 4 is the one now in controversy, legal title to which was i
2 CONC n Fannie Scherer at the time of the commencement of the original actio
2 CONC n. The estimated just compensation for the use of such parcel for on
2 CONC e year was $80.
2 CONT
2 CONT The record indicates that Fannie Scherer died and a stipulation was si
2 CONC gned by John Scherer on November 7, 1945 agreeing to the annual renta
2 CONC l of $80.
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 21, 1945 a final judgment and order of distribution was en
2 CONC tered by the Court which ordered payment of three annual rental paymen
2 CONC ts totaling $240 to John Scherer. He also received the fourth annual r
2 CONC ental payment.
2 CONT
2 CONT The lease was renewed annually by order of the Court, the last renewa
2 CONC l occurring on September 6, 1955 which renewed the lease for a perio
2 CONC d of one year commencing October 1, 1955.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah Hecht had acquired title to Parcel No. 4 in August, 1945 by Coun
2 CONC ty Auditor's Deed following a foreclosure in the state court for failu
2 CONC re to pay real estate taxes and the annual rental payments were disbur
2 CONC sed to her commencing with the fifth one. She signed a stipulation o
2 CONC n November 18, 1953 agreeing to an increase in the annual rental fro
2 CONC m $80 to $387.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1954 the Board of Education of Cleveland City School District appro
2 CONC priated a portion of Parcel No. 4 in the Common Pleas Court of Cuyahog
2 CONC a County.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah Hecht died on March 26, 1955.
2 CONT
2 CONT The national emergency proclaimed by the President terminated on Jul
2 CONC y 1, 1953 and the three-year period expired on July 1, 1956 so that th
2 CONC e lease could not be renewed after its expiration on October 1, 1956 f
2 CONC or any additional period under the original Declaration of Taking. Joi
2 CONC nt Resolution July 3, 1952, 66 Stat. 332, as amended by Joint Resoluti
2 CONC on March 31, 1953, 67 Stat. 18, 42 U.S.C.A. § 1521 note.
2 CONT
2 CONT On September 28, 1956, the Commissioner of Public Housing filed a Decl
2 CONC aration of Taking to appropriate the exclusive use of the residue of P
2 CONC arcel No. 4 and other lands for a period of one year commencing Octobe
2 CONC r 1, 1956, with the right to remove all buildings or improvements inst
2 CONC alled or erected by the Government or previously existing. The renta
2 CONC l specified therein was on the same day deposited in the registry of t
2 CONC he Court. An amended complaint was filed September 28, 1956 praying th
2 CONC at the property be condemned and that just compensation be ascertaine
2 CONC d and awarded. A decree was entered on the Declaration of Taking on Oc
2 CONC tober 2, 1956.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Declaration of Taking states that the purpose was to protect the G
2 CONC overnment's investment in the improvements pending the disposition o
2 CONC f them in conformity with the law.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Hecht, individually and as executor of the estate of Sarah Hecht
2 CONC , has filed a motion to vacate the decree entered on the Declaration o
2 CONC f Taking. No authorities were cited in support of the motion. He has a
2 CONC lso filed an answer setting up certain defenses and a counterclaim fo
2 CONC r the use and occupancy of the property since October 1, 1954 in the a
2 CONC mount of $33,125. He claims to be the devisee of Sarah Hecht and as su
2 CONC ch the owner of the residue of Parcel No. 4.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first ground of complaint by the defendant is that the present con
2 CONC demnation was instituted by proceedings in the original condemnation c
2 CONC ase instead of by a separate and independent action.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since each year's renewal was handled by order of Court entered in th
2 CONC e original case, we see no valid reason why the present appropriatio
2 CONC n of use for another *700 year of the same property cannot be similarl
2 CONC y handled.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is next claimed that the final judgment and order of distribution e
2 CONC ntered on November 21, 1945 was void because Sarah Hecht, who was the
2 CONC n the owner of Parcel No. 4, was not a party to the case.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is further claimed that John Scherer who signed a stipulation and r
2 CONC eceived rent under the orders of Court for four years was not the owne
2 CONC r of Parcel No. 4 and, therefore, said final judgment and order of dis
2 CONC tribution was void.
2 CONT
2 CONT The owner of the land at the time of the original taking is not here c
2 CONC omplaining about any defects in the proceeding or any failure to pay r
2 CONC ental to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT We do not see what right defendant Hecht, a subsequent owner, has to c
2 CONC omplain about the payment of rental or defects in the proceeding prio
2 CONC r to his ownership of the premises.
2 CONT
2 CONT The case was lis pendens when Sarah Hecht acquired ownership of the pr
2 CONC operty in 1945 through tax sale. It was her duty to advise the Court o
2 CONC f her acquisition of the property in order to claim the rental to whic
2 CONC h she was entitled as such owner.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Hecht apparently did advise the Government at a later date of he
2 CONC r ownership of the property because the orders of the Court providin
2 CONC g for the distribution of each of the annual rental payments subsequen
2 CONC t to the fourth, includes her name therein and the rentals were in fac
2 CONC t distributed to her.
2 CONT
2 CONT The judgment entered on February 3, 1954 was pursuant to a stipulatio
2 CONC n signed by Mrs. Hecht, which increased her annual rental from $80 t
2 CONC o $387 and ordered the payment to her of $774 representing the tenth a
2 CONC nd eleventh annual rental distributions. The checks for rental which M
2 CONC rs. Hecht received pursuant to the orders of the Court and her signe
2 CONC d stipulation estop her and her husband from questioning the validit
2 CONC y of the original decree. One may not receive money under the order o
2 CONC f the Court and later question the validity of the order under which i
2 CONC t was paid.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is next claimed that the lease expired on July 1, 1956 which was th
2 CONC ree years after the termination of the emergency and that the attempte
2 CONC d renewal beyond that date was unauthorized. The termination of the em
2 CONC ergency and the expiration of the three-year period had no effect on t
2 CONC he existing lease for which the Government had paid rental to Octobe
2 CONC r 1, 1956, but merely prevented further renewal under the original tak
2 CONC ing. Nor did such termination operate as a forfeiture of improvement
2 CONC s erected by the Government on the leased property. The lease continue
2 CONC d until it expired notwithstanding the fact that it could not be furth
2 CONC er renewed under the original order of condemnation.
2 CONT
2 CONT The present proceeding was instituted to prevent the lease from expiri
2 CONC ng and was authorized by Title 42 U.S.C.A. § 1585(a) as amended Augus
2 CONC t 2, 1954 which provided:
2 CONT
2 CONT "(a) The Administrator may continue by lease or condemnation any inter
2 CONC est less than a fee simple in lands heretofore acquired by the Adminis
2 CONC trator for national defense or war housing or for veterans' housing (w
2 CONC hether of permanent or temporary character), or held by any Federal ag
2 CONC ency in connection therewith, and may acquire, by purchase or condemna
2 CONC tion, a fee simple title to or lesser interest in any such lands if th
2 CONC e Administrator determines that the acquisition of such fee simple o
2 CONC r lesser interest is necessary to protect the Government's investmen
2 CONC t or to maintain the improvements constructed thereon, or that the cos
2 CONC t of fulfilling the Government's obligation to restore the property t
2 CONC o its original condition would equal or exceed the cost of acquiring t
2 CONC he title thereto."
2 CONT
2 CONT It is further contended that the Government's rights to renewal were c
2 CONC onditioned on payment of the rental and that no rental was paid or ten
2 CONC dered to *701 Sarah Hecht or Jacob Hecht for the period commencing Oct
2 CONC ober 1, 1954.
2 CONT
2 CONT The registry of this Court shows the deposit of money by the Governmen
2 CONC t for the periods in question as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT 12th Annual Rental  10-22-54   $387.00
2 CONT 13th Annual Rental   8-24-55    387.00
2 CONT
2 CONT Since the Board of Education acquired a portion of the premises by con
2 CONC demnation, there was paid to it the proportionate part of the rental t
2 CONC o which it was entitled and the balance is still in the hands of the C
2 CONC lerk awaiting distribution. If Mr. Hecht produces evidence to the sati
2 CONC sfaction of the Clerk showing his ownership of the land, he will exper
2 CONC ience no difficulty in obtaining all rentals to which he is entitled.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is further contended that the allegations in the Declaration and co
2 CONC mplaint of the purposes of the taking are not true.
2 CONT
2 CONT The statute before quoted gives to the Commissioner of Public Housin
2 CONC g the right to appropriate either the fee or a lesser interest, if h
2 CONC e determines that it is necessary "to protect the Government's investm
2 CONC ent."
2 CONT
2 CONT There is no question here that the Government has an investment in bui
2 CONC ldings and improvements which it erected at its own cost and expense o
2 CONC n the leased premises.
2 CONT
2 CONT If it had not instituted the present proceeding in condemnation, the i
2 CONC mprovements which it erected on the vacant land would have reverted t
2 CONC o the defendant upon the termination of the lease. United States v. 16
2 CONC .747 Acres of Land, D.C., 50 F. Supp. 389.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Government paid rental to the owners for the use of the vacant lan
2 CONC d. The improvements erected by the Government did not belong to the de
2 CONC fendant as the Government reserved the right to remove them. The onl
2 CONC y purpose of the present condemnation was to extend the lease for anot
2 CONC her year so that the Government might realize some salvage on its inve
2 CONC stment in the improvements.
2 CONT
2 CONT We agree with counsel for defendant that the Government has no right t
2 CONC o renew the lease ad infinitum, but this is only the first renewal und
2 CONC er the statute as amended for the purpose of protecting the Government
2 CONC 's investment. The Commissioner of Public Housing has determined the n
2 CONC ecessity of the condemnation for such purpose.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Court would have no right to substitute its judgment for that of t
2 CONC he Commissioner of Public Housing or to interfere with its exercise i
2 CONC n the absence of fraud, bad faith or a clear abuse of discretion, whic
2 CONC h are not present in this case.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is intimated that the Government is trying to help the City of Clev
2 CONC eland in an action pending in the Common Pleas Court of Cuyahoga Count
2 CONC y which the City instituted to condemn the same premises.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is not apparent just how the condemnation action in this Court wil
2 CONC l help the City of Cleveland in its action in the state court, unles
2 CONC s it might prevent defendant from claiming that he owns the improvemen
2 CONC ts which belong to the Government.
2 CONT
2 CONT The fact that the cost of demolition may exceed the salvage value of t
2 CONC he improvements is no ground for denying the condemnation, for the Gov
2 CONC ernment may be able to sell the improvements without demolition. The G
2 CONC overnment would even have the right to appropriate the fee-simple titl
2 CONC e to the land to aid it in protecting its investment so that it coul
2 CONC d sell both the land and the buildings. United States v. Certain Parce
2 CONC ls of Land, D.C., 141 F. Supp. 300.
2 CONT
2 CONT It should be pointed out here that the Government would have no righ
2 CONC t to remove any improvements except those constructed or erected by it
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Finally, the defendant contends that assuming the lease did not expir
2 CONC e until October 1, 1956, the present proceeding was not instituted unt
2 CONC il after that date and was, therefore, too late.
2 CONT
2 CONT *702 The Declaration of Taking was filed on September 28, 1956, and th
2 CONC e money therefor was then deposited in the registry of the Court. Th
2 CONC e title to the estate condemned thereupon vested in the United States
2 CONC . 40 U.S.C.A. § 258a. The amended complaint was filed on September 28
2 CONC , 1956. The proceeding was, therefore, timely commenced even though th
2 CONC e Court did not enter an order on the Declaration of Taking until Octo
2 CONC ber 2, 1956 and the defendant was not served with summons until Octobe
2 CONC r 8, 1956. Rule 3, Federal Rules of Civil Procedure, 28 U.S.C.A.
2 CONT
2 CONT The motion to vacate is, therefore, overruled.
