0 @N4993@ NOTE
1 CONC In 1934, Jacob (Jack) Hecht opened Cedar Gardens a popular restauran
2 CONC t and nightclub on 9706 Cedar Ave. Cleveland. It was previously a nigh
2 CONC tclub, Charm House and Butterfly Inn, a Chinese restaurant.
2 CONT (In a personal family interview, Jack’s son, Manny said he was 16 year
2 CONC s old when the club opened. He worked there on Saturday nights and car
2 CONC ried a gun. Manny said Saturday nights 90% of the clients were white
2 CONC ; Sunday nights 90% were black).
2 CONT Cedar Gardens (affectionately regarded as Cleveland's Cotton Club) off
2 CONC ered Chinese and American food at affordable prices. It was also equip
2 CONC ped to host parties and families. It was the first and largest "Blac
2 CONC k and Tan" (interracial) night club between Chicago and New York. When
2 CONC ever they showed TV of jazz nights it would be his club. The large bas
2 CONC ement room nightclub presented full floor shows with a house band le
2 CONC d by Marion Sears into the 1940s. The club had a band and at least fiv
2 CONC e acts. The musicians at times included Buster Harding, Andy Anderson
2 CONC , Francis Williams, Earle Warren, Freddie Webster, Tadd Dameron, Bul
2 CONC l Moose Jackson and Pee Wee Jackson. Rose Murphy, the Jazz pianist, go
2 CONC t her start at the club. The intermission piano player was Poison Gard
2 CONC ner. Baby Heinz was a wonderful blues singer married to Earl father He
2 CONC inz. From the 1940s until the late '60s, local groups played at Ceda
2 CONC r Gardens.
2 CONT In addition to being listed in the Green Book guides for African Ameri
2 CONC can travelers, Cedar Gardens was particularly important to Cleveland’
2 CONC s drag scene when drag performers were called “female impersonators.
2 CONC ” Cedar Gardens hosted multiple drag performers, primarily throughou
2 CONC t the 1940s. Cedar Gardens’ history with drag performers provides an e
2 CONC arly view of what would eventually evolve into modern drag culture a
2 CONC s the location provided an early place of acceptance and even praise f
2 CONC or drag artists.
2 CONT In 1935, Cedar Gardens began hosting drag performers. One of the earli
2 CONC est, Sam Fouche, went by the stage name Mae West, came to Cedar Garden
2 CONC s from New York, was only supposed to perform for 1 week, but was so p
2 CONC opular the manager convinced her to stay for longer. West was an incre
2 CONC dibly beloved performer at Cedar. A Call and Post article stated “wel
2 CONC l I know a few fellows who tried to date her… or him. During the pas
2 CONC t week the Cedar Gardens Lounge turned away eager fun lovers every nig
2 CONC ht.” Drag performances were accepted at these entertainment venues an
2 CONC d treated as an art form by the local newspaper, this was especially u
2 CONC nique considering that the Call and Post was a more conservative leani
2 CONC ng Black newspaper.
2 CONT https://queerclevelandhistories.org/locations/cedar-gardens/
2 CONT Cedar Gardens closed in 1969 after a fire destroyed the building. It i
2 CONC s deeply tragic that a location important to Cleveland’s and Black an
2 CONC d LGBTQ+ history was lost. Cedar Gardens provided a place for what w
2 CONC e would now call drag artists to perform their acts for an appreciativ
2 CONC e audience. At Cedar Gardens drag was seen as a worthy form of enterta
2 CONC inment and artistry, with its performers gaining high praise for thei
2 CONC r skills.
2 CONT Resources:
2 CONT - “Bobbing Along with Bob Williams.” Call & Post. May 20, 1944.
2 CONT - “Cedar Gardens.” Call & Post. Jan 27, 1934.
2 CONT - “Cedar Gardens’ Stock is Best of Pre-War Brands of Fine Wines, Liquo
2 CONC rs: Good Order for Funsters is Also Feature in Comfort, Convenience.
2 CONC ” Call & Post. Aug 5, 1944.
2 CONT - “Female Impersonator Held Over for Second Week at Cedar Gardens.” Ca
2 CONC ll & Post. Aug 22, 1935.
2 CONT - “Female Impersonator.” Call & Post. Aug 15, 1935.
2 CONT - “Fire Loss 1-no Title.” Call & Post. Mar 29, 1969.
2 CONT - Minor, Valena. “Patrons Laugh at “Night in Nut House” Given for Land
