1 CONC Robert Weiss was born in Carlsbad, Austria-Hungary (now Karlovy Vary
2 CONC , Czech Republic). He was the son of Jakab Weisz, the internationall
2 CONC y renowned Chief Cantor of The Great Synagogue in Carlsbad, one of th
2 CONC e largest in the region and regularly accommodated between 1200-2000 p
2 CONC eople. When Robert was 11 and Jakab was 42, WWI broke out. Jakab was f
2 CONC orced to leave his wife, Robert and two younger daughters behind to jo
2 CONC in the Austria-Hungarian Army as a foot soldier. His unit was quickl
2 CONC y captured; a few months later, he died of typhus in a Serbian POW cam
2 CONC p.
2 CONT In 1915, a year after losing his father, his mother moved the family b
2 CONC ack to her hometown of Vienna where she rejoined her parents, five bro
2 CONC thers and large, extended family. They lived on her small widow’s pens
2 CONC ion from the Carlsbad Synagogue. By the age of 12, Robert playing th
2 CONC e piano in movie theaters during silent films to help support his moth
2 CONC er and sisters.
2 CONT From 1916 to 1922, between the ages of 13 and 18, Robert attended Vien
2 CONC na’s Gymnasium Kundmanngasse, a prestigious science school. After matr
2 CONC iculating to the University of Vienna, he studied chemistry and did th
2 CONC ree years of laboratory research under Professor Wolfgang Josef Pauli
2 CONC , a pioneer of colloid and protein chemistry. Robert earned his doctor
2 CONC ate in protein chemistry in 1931.
2 CONT Astoundingly, Robert simultaneously attended Vienna’s Academy of Musi
2 CONC c and Performing Arts studying the organ, music theory, composition, a
2 CONC nd conducting; he graduated with honors in 1933. He was an operatic co
2 CONC ach at the Music Academy, and a composer, accompanist, choral and orch
2 CONC estral conductor in Vienna’s Jewish community. He was also a private t
2 CONC eacher of piano, harmony, composition and vocals. During this time, h
2 CONC e was close friends with Sigmund Freud's daughter-in-law, Estee Freud
2 CONC . They hosted salons where she’d recite poetry and he'd play the piano
2 CONT He met his future wife, Rose Hecht, in the standing room only sectio
2 CONC n of Vienna’s Volkstheatre during the play "Die unentschuldigt Stunde"
2 CONC .
2 CONT In 1939 he came to the US where he was engaged in similar posts in var
2 CONC ious cities for conservative synagogues. 1957-1967 he was with Templ
2 CONC e Emanuel in Newton, MA as organist; in 1967 he joined Temple Mishka
2 CONC n Tefillah first as the organist; then also as the choir director.
